<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bad Arolsen</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Arolsen</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bad Arolsen is a small town in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. From the 18th century it was the residence of the counts and princes of Waldeck, who built a large baroque palace there, and it remained the capital of the Free State of Waldeck until 1929. It is home to the Arolsen Archives, the international centre for documents on Nazi persecution.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bad-arolsen.jpg" alt="Bad Arolsen" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Innenstadt_Bad_Arolsen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Bad Arolsen is a small town in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>, in the district of Waldeck-Frankenberg, roughly 45 kilometres west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassel">Kassel</a>. Until 1997 it was called simply Arolsen, Bad being the German word for spa. The oak in its civic coat of arms stands for the surrounding woods and indirectly for the fresh air for which the town is known as a climatic spa. It lies on the railway line from Kassel to Korbach.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest documents naming the place date to 1131, when an Augustinian nunnery was founded there under the name Aroldessen. The nunnery was secularised in 1526, and the counts, later princes, of Waldeck converted it into a stately home. It was torn down in 1710 and replaced by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> palace, built between 1713 and 1728 for Friedrich Anton Ulrich, Prince of Waldeck and Pyrmont. The town beside it was laid out on a Baroque chequered grid. A mirror-image layout planned for the east of the palace was never built, because the money ran out after the western part was done.</p><p>From 1918 to 1929 Arolsen was the capital of the Free State of Waldeck-Pyrmont, called the Free State of Waldeck after 1922, which was then incorporated into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. From 1943 to 1945 the Nazis ran a subcamp of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buchenwald-concentration-camp">Buchenwald concentration camp</a> there, which held 185 prisoners in all. After the war the town lay in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/occupation-of-japan">American occupation</a> zone and became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-germany">West Germany</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Since 1946 Bad Arolsen has been the headquarters of the International Tracing Service, which searches for civilians lost to their families through war or political unrest during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. Its archives hold vast numbers of documents on Nazi persecution, and its records were opened to the public in November 2007. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> palace survives, with ceiling paintings by Carlo Ludovici Castelli, and so does the Grosse Allee, a broad avenue about a mile long lined with some 880 oaks, laid out in 1676. The sculptor Christian Daniel Rauch, born in 1777, was a native of the town.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bad Arolsen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Prince Friedrich of Waldeck-Pyrmont was deposed in November 1918, and the principality became a free state with <b>Arolsen</b> as capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-state-of-waldeck">Free State of Waldeck</a></li><li><blockquote>The counts, raised to princes in 1712, ruled a poor, rural land of small towns from <b>Arolsen</b>, where they built a baroque palace.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-waldeck">Principality of Waldeck</a></li></ul></section>
